**Convention 9.2 (arbitrary competitors and exact cardinality).** An
arbitrary original competitor first undergoes the credited RRN Theorem
7.1 normalization. It may reduce $m$ attained names to $m_0\le m$;
it preserves the optimum exactly, so $m_0=m=M_1(D)$ for an optimal
competitor. Theorem 9.1 then preserves this canonical assignment and
its complete alphabet exactly. It does not assert preservation of the
original competitor's unnormalized fibers or preparation cost.

If exactly $m$ names of a nonminimal competitor are required, RRN
Corollary 7.2 refines assigned fibers and duplicates their valid public
witnesses. Its fuller scan has at most $2^{D+1}-1$ Reads, maximum
length $D+1$ and $D2^{D+1}+1$ letters. Contraction preserves those
refined names as well. No uncharged source-dependent cut, phase,
shape tag or topology selector is introduced by either correspondence.

